  [Escalation] Conflict summary:
    A 20% demand surge has produced a schedule that violates two human-centric constraints simultaneously. FatigueIndex reached 0.6001 against a hard ceiling of 0.40 (overage: +0.2001, or 50% above the limit), and workplace Noise reached 84.9 dB against a ceiling of 80.0 dB (overage: +4.9 dB). Because both violations concern worker safety and wellbeing, the CBPA framework has blocked automatic approval and escalated to governance. No feasible schedule exists under current resource and constraint parameters that can absorb the full 20% demand increase without exceeding at least one human-centric bound. Three ranked remediation options are presented below for production-manager decision; all require explicit human authorization before re-scheduling is attempted.

  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 (Preferred) — Demand Phasing with Added Shift Capacity: Defer approximately 12–15% of the incremental demand to the next planning window and authorize one additional partial shift staffed with rested operators. This keeps both FatigueIndex and Noise within their approved limits while recovering the majority of demand within the current period. Residual unmet demand (~5–8% of the 20% surge) is backlogged to the next cycle. Recommend reviewing upstream order commitments to negotiate a short lead-time extension with customers.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 (Moderate) — Targeted Noise Relaxation with Mandatory PPE Upgrade: Relax the Noise ceiling from 80.0 dB to 85.0 dB for the duration of this planning window, contingent on: (a) issuing Class-A hearing protection (NRR ≥ 25) to all floor personnel, (b) reducing continuous noise-exposure intervals by 30% via mandatory micro-breaks, and (c) scheduling audiometric follow-up within 10 working days. FatigueIndex must still be addressed by capping per-operator daily output at 90% of current projection and cross-training two support workers onto bottleneck stations. This combination is estimated to close the Noise gap fully and reduce the FatigueIndex overage from 0.2001 to roughly 0.05, leaving a small residual deadline gap.
      Preserves human constraints: No
    - Option 3 (Last Resort) — Dual Constraint Relaxation with Compensatory Recovery Plan: Simultaneously raise the Noise ceiling to 85.0 dB (with full PPE mandate) and raise the FatigueIndex ceiling to 0.55 for a single planning window. This allows the full 20% demand increase to be absorbed on schedule. Mandatory compensatory measures must be contractually committed before scheduling begins: (a) a 3-day reduced-intensity period immediately following this window, (b) mandatory rest-day scheduling for any operator whose personal FatigueIndex exceeds 0.50 during the window, and (c) written acknowledgment from the plant safety officer. This option carries the highest residual health and compliance risk and should only be selected if customer penalty costs demonstrably exceed the total cost of compensatory and medical-monitoring measures.
      Preserves human constraints: No

  [Manager] Decision: Option C
    Rationale: Prioritizing human well-being over deadline
