    3. priority_order vs _raw_priority_order conflict
       Q: The contract lists HumanWellbeing as the sole lexicographic priority, yet the raw intent order places delivered_value_per_resource first — should the optimizer treat fatigue and noise purely as hard constraints (optimize efficiency within their bounds) or apply a strict lexicographic preference where any marginal improvement to wellbeing overrides efficiency gains?
       A: Treat fatigue ≤ 0.4 and noise ≤ 80 dB as inviolable hard constraints, then maximize delivered_value_per_resource within that feasible region — i.e., wellbeing defines the boundary, efficiency drives the objective.
    4. operator_fatigue / temporal interpretation of 0.4 threshold
       Q: Does the 0.4 fatigue limit apply to the instantaneous score at every monitoring interval, the shift-peak (maximum recorded value), or the time-weighted average across the shift — since an instantaneous cap prevents all burst exposure while an average cap could allow fatigue spikes above 0.6 for short periods?
       A: Apply the 0.4 limit as a rolling 30-minute peak (the worst value within any 30-minute window must not exceed 0.4), balancing protection against sustained high load while permitting brief transient variation.
    5. noise_level / temporal and spatial measurement basis
       Q: Is the 80 dB limit an 8-hour time-weighted average (matching OSHA TWA methodology), a continuous ambient floor reading, or a never-exceed instantaneous peak at the nearest operator position — since scheduling noisy machine operations in bursts can satisfy a shift average while exposing operators to peaks above 90 dB for extended sub-periods?
       A: Apply 80 dB as the OSHA-equivalent 8-hour time-weighted average at the operator position, with an additional hard cap of 90 dB instantaneous peak to prevent burst exposure harm.

