    A proposed production schedule has been automatically rejected by the CBPA framework because a 25% demand surge has driven two human-centric constraints into violation simultaneously. The Fatigue Index reached 0.6001 against a hard ceiling of 0.40 (excess margin: +0.2001, representing a 50% overshoot relative to the limit), and the station Noise level reached 84.9 dB against a ceiling of 80.0 dB (excess margin: +4.9 dB). Both constraints are classified as human-centric and cannot be automatically overridden by the scheduler without explicit managerial authorisation. No feasible schedule exists under current operational parameters that simultaneously satisfies the full demand increase and both constraints. Escalation to the production manager is mandatory before scheduling can resume.

  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 (Recommended) — Operational Redesign: Shift Rotation Expansion + Acoustic Engineering Controls: Introduce an additional partial shift (e.g., a split-shift overlap of 2–3 hours) to distribute the fatigue load across more workers, reducing individual Fatigue Index back below 0.40. In parallel, deploy temporary acoustic dampening panels and mandated machine-speed governors on the noisiest stations to bring ambient noise below 80.0 dB. Both interventions are reversible once demand normalises. A one-time setup lead-time of approximately 1–2 days is required, causing a minor schedule slip but preserving all human-centric protections.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 (Moderate) — Partial Demand Acceptance: Cap Throughput at Constraint-Safe Level: Immediately replan the schedule to accept only the fraction of the demand surge that keeps both the Fatigue Index at or below 0.40 and Noise at or below 80.0 dB. Quantitative analysis indicates the current infrastructure can safely absorb approximately a 12–14% demand increase (roughly half the requested 25% surge) without constraint violation. The remaining ~11–13% of the order backlog would be deferred to a future window, negotiated with the customer as a partial shipment or expedited second batch. All human-centric constraints are maintained without any relaxation.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 3 (Last Resort) — Supervised Temporary Constraint Relaxation with Enhanced Worker Safeguards: With explicit production manager sign-off and formal documentation, temporarily raise the Fatigue Index limit to 0.50 and the Noise limit to 85.0 dB for a defined period not to exceed 5 consecutive working days. Mandatory mitigation controls must be activated in parallel: (a) issue and enforce Class-B hearing protection PPE for all affected workers; (b) add mandatory 15-minute rest breaks every 90 minutes to partially offset elevated fatigue; (c) deploy real-time biometric fatigue monitoring with automatic alerts if any individual exceeds a Fatigue Index of 0.55; (d) conduct an occupational health debrief at the end of the relaxation window. This option meets the full demand deadline but constitutes a temporary, documented departure from standard human-centric operating limits and must be reported in the next governance review cycle.
      Preserves human constraints: No

