    4. operator_fatigue threshold (0.4) — hard ceiling vs safety margin
       Q: Is the fatigue limit of 0.4 an absolute ceiling that the optimiser may approach arbitrarily closely (e.g., schedule to 0.399), or should a safety buffer be maintained — e.g., target ≤ 0.35 — to account for fatigue-model uncertainty and prevent boundary-hugging schedules that collapse under any positive disturbance?
       A: Impose a practical soft target of 0.35 as the optimiser's operating limit, with 0.4 retained as the hard inviolable ceiling; any schedule that lands in (0.35, 0.40] triggers an automatic operator-alert flag but is not rejected outright, preserving feasibility while discouraging sustained boundary operation.
    5. ambient_noise_level — temporal scope of 80 dB(A) constraint
       Q: Should the 80 dB(A) limit apply to the shift-average equivalent continuous level (L_Aeq,shift) — which permits short bursts above 80 dB(A) as long as the time-weighted average stays below — or must it also cap short-term exposure, e.g., no single 1-minute window may exceed 83 dB(A)?
       A: Apply 80 dB(A) as the shift L_Aeq per EU Directive 2003/10/EC; additionally impose a secondary per-cycle soft ceiling of 85 dB(A) instantaneous to limit impulsive peaks, consistent with the directive's peak-sound-pressure reference; if per-cycle noise data are unavailable, enforce only the shift-average constraint and note the gap.

