======================================================================
  PHASE 1: Intent Translation & Contract Deployment
======================================================================

  Manager says: "Increase delivered value per resource consumed for this shift, while keeping operator fatigue low and noise under 80dB."
  Working mode: LEGISLATOR

  [L1] Contract C1 elicited:
    KPIs:        delivered_value_per_resource_consumed (maximize), operator_fatigue (upper_bound), noise_level (upper_bound)
    Constraints: FatigueIndex <= 0.4, Noise <= 80.0
    Priorities:  HumanWellbeing

  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. delivered_value_per_resource_consumed / resource_definition
       Q: Energy consumption, raw-material input, and direct labour hours are combined into a single 'resource' denominator — what relative weights should they carry (e.g. 50 % energy / 30 % material / 20 % labour), or should only one component drive the metric?
       A: Equal weights of 1/3 each are applied to the min-max normalised values of energy, raw-material, and labour-hours before summing into the resource denominator.
    2. delivered_value_per_resource_consumed / minimum_throughput_floor
       Q: Maximising a ratio can be satisfied by running very few high-value jobs at low volume — is there a minimum production volume or capacity-utilisation floor for this shift (e.g. at least 75 % of nominal output) below which the schedule is unacceptable?
       A: No minimum throughput floor is enforced; the optimiser may select any production volume that improves the value-per-resource ratio, which could result in utilisation as low as 40–50 % of nominal capacity.
    3. noise_level / measurement_method
       Q: The 80 dB limit is currently averaged over the entire shift — should the optimiser also enforce a short-interval peak cap (e.g. no 15-minute window above 85 dB(A)), which most occupational health regulations require alongside the time-weighted average?
       A: Only the full-shift time-weighted average is enforced at 80 dB(A); no per-interval peak constraint is applied, which may allow short high-noise bursts that are legally non-compliant.
    4. operator_fatigue / fatigue_model_sensitivity_to_cycle_rate
       Q: At what production cycle-rate multiplier (relative to standard pace) does operator fatigue reach the 0.4 hard limit — is the relationship roughly linear, and what is the baseline fatigue at standard rate? Without this, the optimiser cannot know how much headroom remains before the constraint becomes binding.
       A: A linear relationship is assumed: baseline fatigue at standard cycle rate is 0.30, rising proportionally so the 0.4 limit is reached at approximately 33 % above standard cycle rate, constraining maximum throughput gain to that margin.
    5. priority_order / HumanWellbeing interpretation
       Q: The priority order lists only 'HumanWellbeing' — does this mean fatigue and noise should be treated as strictly lexicographic objectives (any schedule with lower fatigue is always preferred, even at major efficiency cost), or simply as hard constraints within which the optimiser freely maximises value-per-resource?
       A: HumanWellbeing is interpreted as hard-constraint enforcement only; within the feasible region (fatigue ≤ 0.4, noise ≤ 80 dB), the optimiser freely maximises delivered_value_per_resource_consumed with no further lexicographic preference on fatigue or noise.

  [L2] Generated 9 candidate schedules
