    A 20.0% demand surge has produced an infeasible production schedule that violates two human-centric constraints simultaneously. The proposed schedule yields a FatigueIndex of 0.6001 (limit: 0.40, excess: +0.2001) and a Noise level of 84.9 dB (limit: 80.0 dB, excess: +4.9 dB). Both violations are significant: the fatigue overrun is 50% above the allowed ceiling, raising acute ergonomic and safety risk, while the noise overrun exceeds the 80 dB occupational-exposure threshold, triggering mandatory hearing-protection requirements under most regulatory frameworks. No schedule that fully absorbs the demand increase can be generated without explicit human-in-the-loop authorisation to modify at least one constraint boundary. Immediate escalation and a governance decision are required before production can resume at the elevated demand level.

  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 — Demand Deferral (Constraint-Preserving): Accept only the portion of demand that can be satisfied within current FatigueIndex (≤0.40) and Noise (≤80.0 dB) limits. Approximately 20% of the incremental orders are deferred to the next scheduling cycle or redistributed to an alternate facility. No human-centric constraint is modified. Requires immediate customer communication regarding partial on-time delivery and coordination with logistics for split shipments.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 — Moderate Temporary Relaxation with Engineering Controls: Temporarily raise the FatigueIndex ceiling to 0.50 and the Noise ceiling to 82.0 dB for a defined short-term window (e.g., one shift or 48 hours), contingent on compensatory controls: mandatory micro-rest intervals every 90 minutes, provision of Class B hearing protection (SNR ≥ 27 dB) for all operators, and real-time fatigue monitoring with automatic line-stop at 0.50. A safety officer must countersign the relaxation order. This option recovers approximately half the demand gap while limiting residual human-welfare risk.
      Preserves human constraints: No
    - Option 3 — Full Constraint Relaxation to Meet Demand (Highest Risk): Raise the FatigueIndex ceiling to 0.65 and the Noise ceiling to 85.0 dB to allow a schedule that absorbs the full 20% demand increase without deferral. This option eliminates the deadline gap entirely but requires written executive sign-off, a formal risk-acceptance record, enhanced PPE deployment (Class A hearing protection, SNR ≥ 33 dB), post-shift medical checks, and immediate notification to the site safety committee. Sustained operation at these levels is not recommended beyond a single production run. Regulatory disclosure may be required depending on jurisdiction.
      Preserves human constraints: No

  [Manager] Decision: Option C
    Rationale: Prioritizing human well-being over deadline
