  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. operator_fatigue – temporal interpretation of threshold
       Q: Is the fatigue ≤ 0.4 limit a continuous peak constraint enforced at every moment of the shift, or a cumulative / end-of-shift average? (If it is an average, the optimiser is permitted to let fatigue spike to ~0.7 mid-shift as long as it recovers by handover.)
       A: Apply as a continuous hard ceiling: fatigue must remain ≤ 0.4 at all sampled time-steps throughout the shift, with no recovery averaging.
    2. context – shift_duration (missing)
       Q: What is the planned shift length in hours (e.g., 8 h standard vs 10 h or 12 h extended)? Fatigue accumulates non-linearly over time, so the feasibility of holding fatigue ≤ 0.4 while maximising value_per_resource changes significantly between an 8 h and a 12 h shift.
       A: Assume a standard 8-hour shift; if actual duration differs, the fatigue model parameters must be rescaled before the optimiser runs.
    3. machine_speed – missing upper bound and noise-speed coupling
       Q: At what machine_speed setting (rpm or %) does ambient noise reach 80 dB(A), and is there a mechanical or safety maximum speed cap? Without this, the optimiser will drive speed to the noise ceiling with zero headroom for transient peaks or multi-machine overlap.
       A: Apply a 2 dB(A) safety margin: cap machine operation so the modelled time-weighted average stays ≤ 78 dB(A), preserving headroom for unmodelled transient events.
    4. priority_order vs _raw_priority_order conflict
       Q: The stated priority_order lists only 'HumanWellbeing', but the raw elicitation order puts value_per_resource first. Should the optimiser maximise value_per_resource as the sole primary objective (with fatigue and noise only as hard constraints), or should it actively minimise fatigue/noise as co-objectives even when both hard constraints are already satisfied?
       A: Treat operator_fatigue ≤ 0.4 and noise_level ≤ 80 dB(A) as inviolable hard constraints; maximise value_per_resource as the single lexicographic primary objective within the feasible region, with no further minimisation of fatigue or noise beyond constraint compliance.
    5. value_per_resource – no per-resource ceiling in aggregated denominator
       Q: Because all resources (labor, energy, material, uptime) are collapsed into one cost denominator, the optimiser may push a single resource type—most likely energy via high machine speeds—to its physical limit while reducing others, achieving a high ratio through an operationally unsustainable configuration. Should any individual resource have a shift-level cap (e.g., maximum energy_kWh per shift)?
       A: Apply no individual resource cap; rely on the cost weighting embedded in resource_definition to implicitly penalise over-concentration. Flag for manual review if any single monitored resource exceeds 120% of its historical shift baseline.

