  [Escalation] Conflict summary:
    A 15.0% demand surge has rendered the proposed production schedule infeasible under current human-centric safety constraints. Two violations were detected: (1) FatigueIndex reached 0.6001, exceeding the 0.40 ceiling by a margin of 0.2001 — indicating workers would be exposed to unsustainable physical strain; (2) Noise level reached 84.9 dB, exceeding the 80.0 dB limit by 4.9 dB — a meaningful auditory health risk under sustained exposure. Both constraints are classified as human-centric and cannot be automatically overridden without explicit managerial authorization. Immediate intervention is required to either reduce throughput targets, add production capacity, or negotiate a time-limited, safeguarded relaxation of one or both thresholds.

  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 (Recommended): Partial Demand Deferral with Load Leveling: Accept the 15% demand increase but distribute it across an extended horizon by deferring approximately 10–12% of the uplift to the next scheduling window. The remaining 3–5% uplift can be absorbed by existing capacity through minor line-balancing adjustments. No constraint thresholds are modified. Workers remain within both the fatigue and noise limits. A customer communication plan should accompany this option to manage delivery expectations.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 (Moderate): Engineering Controls + Shift Redistribution: Deploy acoustic dampening panels and anti-vibration mounts at high-noise workstations to reduce ambient Noise to ≤80.0 dB. Simultaneously, split the current shift structure into shorter, higher-frequency rotations (e.g., 4×2h micro-shifts with mandatory 20-minute recovery breaks) to bring FatigueIndex to ≤0.40. These measures require a 2–3 day equipment installation lead time and revised scheduling logic. Full demand fulfillment is achievable by day 4 of the new cycle. Capital cost estimate: moderate (engineering controls) plus scheduling overhead.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 3 (Last Resort): Time-Limited Constraint Relaxation with Mandatory Safeguards: Temporarily raise the FatigueIndex ceiling to 0.50 and the Noise ceiling to 83.0 dB for a maximum of 5 consecutive working days, subject to: (a) mandatory enhanced PPE (ISO-rated ear protection reducing effective exposure by ≥5 dB), (b) 30-minute mandatory rest breaks every 90 minutes of high-demand work, (c) real-time biometric monitoring with automatic line stoppage if FatigueIndex exceeds 0.55, and (d) written informed consent from all affected workers. This option requires formal sign-off from the production manager, safety officer, and HR. Violation of the temporary limits during this window triggers immediate escalation. This option partially compromises the original human-centric constraint guarantees and is not recommended unless Options 1 and 2 are operationally unavailable.
      Preserves human constraints: No

  [Manager] Decision: Option C
    Rationale: Prioritizing human well-being over deadline
