    - Option 1 — Add Supplementary Shift Capacity (Recommended): Distribute the 15% demand increase across an additional partial shift or a rotating overtime pool rather than intensifying existing shifts. By spreading workload over more worker-hours, both FatigueIndex and noise exposure per worker return within compliant bounds. Requires scheduling additional personnel and coordinating shift handoffs. This is the only option that meets full demand without any constraint relaxation or deadline impact.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 — Partial Demand Fulfillment with Deferral: Accept only the portion of the demand increase (approximately 8%) that keeps FatigueIndex at or below 0.40 and noise below 80.0 dB. Defer the remaining ~7% of demand to a subsequent production window or negotiate a revised delivery date with the customer. All worker safety constraints remain fully intact. Recommended when additional staffing cannot be arranged within the required lead time.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 3 — Temporary Constraint Relaxation with Mandatory Compensatory Controls (Last Resort): Temporarily raise the FatigueIndex ceiling to 0.50 and the noise limit to 85.0 dB for a single production run, subject to mandatory compensatory controls: enforced micro-rest breaks every 45 minutes, mandatory ISO-rated hearing protection (SNR ≥ 25 dB) for all floor personnel, and a post-run occupational health check. This option meets the full 15% demand increase with no deadline gap but constitutes a formal deviation from human-centric policy and requires written sign-off from the production manager and safety officer before activation. The deviation must be logged in the CBPA audit trail.
