  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 — Expand Operational Capacity (Recommended): Add a second shift or distribute incremental demand across additional certified workers to reduce per-worker fatigue accumulation and aggregate noise exposure. Concurrently, deploy supplemental acoustic dampening panels or enforce mandatory quiet-zone rotations so that no station exceeds 80 dB for the entire shift. This approach eliminates both violations at the root cause — excessive load per worker — without relaxing any safety limit. Upfront coordination with HR and facilities is required. Expected lead time: 1–3 days.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 — Partial Demand Deferral to Next Production Cycle: Accept and schedule only the volume that can be produced within the existing safety envelopes (approximately 80% of the uplifted demand, equivalent to the original baseline). Defer the remaining 20–25% increment to the next available production window or negotiate a revised delivery commitment with the customer. This option requires no operational changes and guarantees full constraint compliance immediately. The trade-off is a measurable delivery shortfall that must be communicated to downstream stakeholders without delay.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 3 — Supervised Temporary Constraint Relaxation (Last Resort, Requires Sign-Off): With explicit written approval from the Production Manager and EHS officer, temporarily raise the FatigueIndex ceiling from 0.40 to 0.48 and the Noise ceiling from 80.0 dB to 82.5 dB for a defined window not to exceed 5 consecutive working days. Mandatory compensatory controls must be in force: enforced 15-minute rest breaks every 90 minutes, personal hearing-protection rated ≥ 3 dB NRR issued to all affected workers, and daily fatigue self-assessments logged. A return-to-standard review must be scheduled at the end of the window. This option partially meets the demand increase (~90% fulfillment) but knowingly operates outside baseline human-welfare thresholds and exposes the organisation to elevated duty-of-care liability.
      Preserves human constraints: No

