    A proposed production schedule to accommodate a 20.0% demand surge has been automatically rejected by the CBPA governance layer because it violates two human-centric constraints simultaneously. The worker Fatigue Index reached 0.6001, exceeding the approved ceiling of 0.40 by a margin of 0.2001 (approximately 50% over limit). Ambient Noise reached 84.9 dB, exceeding the approved ceiling of 80.0 dB by 4.9 dB. Under the CBPA framework, human-centric constraints are non-negotiable by default and require explicit managerial authorisation before any relaxation can be enacted. No feasible schedule exists that meets full incremental demand within current constraint envelopes. Immediate intervention is required to either reduce effective demand, increase capacity, or formally authorise a time-bounded constraint relaxation with compensatory controls.

  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 (Recommended) — Partial Demand Deferral: Defer approximately 17% of the incremental demand volume (roughly the portion above what current-compliant capacity can absorb) to the next production window. Schedule compliant orders immediately; flag deferred orders for expedited processing once capacity is restored or augmented. This requires coordination with sales/logistics to renegotiate delivery commitments for the deferred subset. No human-centric constraint is relaxed; worker safety and wellbeing are fully preserved.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 (Moderate) — Augment Workforce with Noise-Engineering Controls: Introduce a supplementary worker rotation scheme (e.g., split-shift or additional crew) to distribute task load and reduce per-worker fatigue exposure. Simultaneously deploy targeted noise-dampening measures (acoustic enclosures, anti-vibration mounts, mandatory PPE) to bring ambient Noise back within the 80 dB ceiling. Modelling indicates this can bring FatigueIndex to approximately 0.39 and Noise to approximately 79.5 dB while absorbing up to 85% of the demand increase. A residual ~3–5% of orders may still require short deferrals during the ramp-up period for equipment installation and crew onboarding.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 3 (Last Resort) — Time-Bounded Constraint Relaxation with Compensatory Controls: Request formal managerial and safety-officer authorisation to temporarily raise the FatigueIndex ceiling to 0.50 and the Noise ceiling to 82.5 dB for a defined period (recommended: not to exceed 5 consecutive working days). Mandatory compensatory controls must accompany this relaxation: enforced micro-break schedules every 90 minutes, continuous real-time biometric monitoring with automated alerts at 90% of the new thresholds, provision of Class-A hearing protection rated to attenuate 82.5 dB exposure, and a post-period occupational health review for all affected workers. This option meets 100% of demand with no delivery gap but carries elevated residual health risk and must be logged as a formal governance exception in the CBPA audit trail.
      Preserves human constraints: No

  [Manager] Decision: Option C
    Rationale: Prioritizing human well-being over deadline
