  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 (Recommended): Expand Workforce and Deploy Acoustic Mitigation Equipment: Onboard temporary or redeployed workers to distribute the increased workload, reducing per-worker duty burden and bringing FatigueIndex back within the 0.40 limit. Concurrently procure and install acoustic baffling, vibration-damping mounts, or enclosures on the highest-noise machinery to reduce ambient levels below 80.0 dB. This option requires no relaxation of any human-centric constraint and fully preserves worker safety. Estimated implementation lead time: 3–5 business days for staffing augmentation; 5–10 business days for noise engineering controls. A partial demand gap is expected only during the implementation window.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 (Moderate): Supervised Partial Relaxation with Mandatory Occupational Controls: Temporarily relax FatigueIndex to 0.50 and Noise ceiling to 82.0 dB, contingent on the following mandatory mitigations: (a) enforced worker rotation with a maximum 4-hour continuous duty cycle and minimum 30-minute inter-shift rest; (b) mandatory provision and verified use of ISO-rated hearing protection (minimum SNR 10 dB) for all floor personnel; (c) daily occupational health check-ins by a designated safety officer; (d) a formal constraint-relaxation waiver co-signed by the production manager and EHS officer, logged in the CBPA audit trail. This option can be activated within 24 hours and is expected to close approximately 22 percentage points of the 25% demand gap. Constraints remain non-compliant with baseline policy until reverted.
      Preserves human constraints: No
    - Option 3 (Conservative Fallback): Partial Demand Deferral — Preserve All Constraints Unconditionally: Accept current human-centric constraint limits as inviolable and fulfill only the share of demand achievable within a fully compliant schedule. The remaining demand shortfall (approximately 25%) is renegotiated with customers as a deferred delivery or split across future production cycles. No constraint limit is modified; worker fatigue and noise exposure remain within approved thresholds. This option carries customer satisfaction risk and potential contractual penalties but eliminates all occupational health and regulatory liability. Recommended when downstream contract flexibility exists or when workforce safety is a non-negotiable organizational priority.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes

